<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kaithal State</h1><p class="st-meta">1767 CE – 1843 CE (approx.) · capital Kaithal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sikh chiefship ruled by the Bhai family, which lapsed to the British in 1843 when its last chief died without an heir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bhais of Kaithal descended from Bhai Bhagtu, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> devotee honoured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus">Gurus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desu-singh">Desu Singh</a>, of that family, seized Kaithal around 1767 in the Sikh expansion across the former Mughal province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kaithal was one of the larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cis-sutlej-states">Cis-Sutlej</a> chiefships and accepted British protection in 1809. Its chiefs were known by the title Bhai. The state is thinly documented beyond its succession disputes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Bhai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-of-kaithal">Udai Singh</a> died childless in 1843, the British annexed most of Kaithal, and resistance by local supporters was put down. The town became a British district headquarters and is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kaithal-state.jpg" alt="Kaithal State" width="320" height="370"><figcaption>Image: Cartographers and surveyors of the British East India Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_the_main,_continuous_tract_of_territory_of_Kaithal_State_from_a_map_created_by_the_British_East_India_Company_of_the_Malwa_region_of_Punjab_showing_the_various_polities,_borders,_and_settlements_of_the_area,_ca.1829%E2%80%931835.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1767 CE – 1843 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaithal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Desu Singh, Udai Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism; Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Haryanvi, Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaithal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1805&amp;focus=kaithal-state" aria-label="Kaithal State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6667 1089 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kaithal State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6987 1345L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6987 1345h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6987 1345h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kaithal. <a href="/atlas?year=1805&amp;focus=kaithal-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

